<handout xml:id="pp-8">
    <title>Portfolio Problem 8</title>

  <p>
    All problems are in an Overleaf document at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3tn"/>. Copy/paste problems
    from there to learn notation in Overleaf!

    The writing guidelines are at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3to"/>.

    There's a sample portfolio solution at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3v0"/>.

    There's an Overleaf Cheat Sheet at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3OL"/>.

    All of the writing guidelines are at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3NG"></url>.

    All of the proof portfolio objectives are at
    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3QC"></url>.
  </p>

<p>
    <alert>Instructions:</alert>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                Choose ONE of the following theorems and prove.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Both conjectures talk about irrational numbers.
                Be sure to clearly state the definition of a rational number.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Both conjectures require a ``lemma'' that you should state,
                prove, and then cite in the proof of your theorem.
                If you aren't sure what lemma you should use, please ask Dr.
                Keough (keoulaur@gvsu.edu).
                Recall that our AI policy on the proof portfolio is do not use,
                ever.
                Talk to DocK!
                She wants to help!
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                The order in the typed revision is Lemma, Proof of lemma,
                Theorem, Proof of theorem.
                Use the lemma, theorem, and proof environments in Overleaf:
            </p>
            <pre>
        \begin{lemma} ... \end{lemma}
        \begin{proof} ... \end{proof}
        \begin{theorem} ... \end{theorem}
        \begin{proof} ... \end{proof}
            </pre>
            <p>
                If you plan to use a definition, don't put it between a
                statement and its proof.
                You can put it between the proof of the lemma and the theorem
                statement (and I would if you use that definition in the theorem
                or theorem proof, but not in the lemma or lemma proof.)
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                In your typed revision, put a special focus on communicating
                proof techniques you are using clearly.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</p>

<p>
    <alert>Theorem 8A.</alert> The real number <m>\log_{3} 8</m> is irrational.
    (Recall that <m>\log_{3} 8</m> is a real number <m>a</m> such that
    <m>3^{a} = 8</m>.)
    <!--Lemma hint: Show that  any power of $8$ is even and any power of $3$ is odd.-->
</p>

<p>
    <alert>Theorem 8B.</alert> The real number <m>\sqrt[3]{2}</m> is irrational.
    <!--You'll need to show that a^3 is even if and only if a is even. -->
</p>

<p>
    <alert>Overleaf/LaTex Notes:</alert>
</p>

<p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                To get <m>\log_{3}(8)</m> type
            </p>
            <pre>
    $\log_3(8)$
            </pre>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                To get <m>\sqrt[3]{2}</m> type
            </p>
            <pre>
    $\sqrt[3]{2}$
            </pre>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                To get a fraction like <m>\frac{x}{y}</m> or a bigger fraction
                like <m>\dfrac{x}{y}</m> type
            </p>
            <pre>
    $\frac{x}{y}$ or $\dfrac{x}{y}$
            </pre>
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>
</handout>